{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2503.05705, how does scaling inference-at-deployment affect both the number of simultaneously served model copies and the value of securing model weights?", "answer": "Scaling inference-at-deployment reduces the number of simultaneously served copies and lowers the value of securing model weights, since high inference costs dominate and stolen weights are less useful.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "A shift to scaling inference-at-deployment would lower this number. e.g. if inference-at-deployment is scaled by two orders of magnitude, then this key parameter goes down by a factor of 100, and the new model can only be immediately deployed in 1% as many tasks as it would be if it had been scaled by pre-training compute."}, {"page": 5, "quote": "Then the value of stealing model weights hasn't increased over time - it is just the value of not having to train a GPT-4 level model (which has been decreasing over time by about 4x per year due to algorithmic efficiency improvements and Moore's law (Epoch AI 2024)). And even if the weights were stolen, the thief would still have to pay the high inference-at-deployment costs."}]},
  {"question": "What are two major implications for AI governance if inference scaling is used during training, as described in arXiv 2503.05705?", "answer": "Inference scaling during training can enable continued pre-training via synthetic data and may allow rapid capability growth through iterated distillation and amplification.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "An obvious approach to scaling inference-during-training is to use an inference-scaled model to generate large amounts of high-quality synthetic data on which to pre-train a new base model."}, {"page": 12, "quote": "So iterated distillation and amplification provides a plausible pathway for scaling inference-during-training to rapidly create much more powerful AI systems. Arguably this would constitute a form of 'recursive self-improvement' where AI systems are applied to the task of improving their own capabilities, leading to a rapid escalation."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2503.05705, what does the abbreviation 'OOM' stand for, and how is it used in estimating model capabilities?", "answer": "'OOM' stands for 'orders of magnitude' and is used in formulas estimating model capabilities by combining pre-training and inference compute.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "Effective orders of magnitude = OOMs of pre-training + 0.7 × OOMs of inference to estimate the capabilities of an inference-scaled model in terms of the familiar yardstick of pure pre-training."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2503.05705, what is the main limitation currently preventing models from achieving much higher performance solely by scaling inference-at-deployment?", "answer": "Current techniques for inference scaling hit plateaus that cannot be exceeded by simply increasing inference compute, requiring further research to overcome.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "The main limitation on this at the moment is that many current techniques for inference scaling seem to hit plateaus that can't be exceeded by any level of inference scale-up. Exceeding these plateaus requires substantial research time by AI scientists and engineers, such that if someone tried to use a GPT-4 level model with 100,000x the inference compute, it may not be able to make good use of most of that compute."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2503.05705, what change in the AI industry structure is expected if most compute scaling shifts to inference-at-deployment?", "answer": "Shifting compute scaling to inference-at-deployment could disrupt the current software-like business model, potentially allowing more room for smaller players.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "But if the next two orders of magnitude of compute scale-up go into inference-at-deployment instead of into pre-training, then this would change, upsetting the existing business model and perhaps allowing more room for smaller players in the industry."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2503.05705, what is the effect of scaling inference-at-deployment on the ability of wealthier users to obtain better AI outputs?", "answer": "Scaling inference-at-deployment enables wealthier users to pay for better AI outputs, ending the previous norm of equal-quality access for all users.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "A related change is that users with more money will likely be able to convert that into better answers. We've already seen this start to happen at OpenAI (the first frontier company to allow access to a model that scales inference-at-deployment). They now charge 10x as much for access to the version using the most inferencecompute. ... But scaling inference-at-deployment ends that."}]}
]}
